22. Operator composition

Pantheon operators should no longer be treated as independent tools.

They form an operator algebra.

For example:

ControlledAmnesia
\circ
AlienLane
\circ
PatternSansEchoes

tests whether a structure independently regenerates.

GoalSeek
\circ
Goldilocks
\circ
ReversibleInteraction

finds the smallest survivable intervention capable of moving a system toward a target.

GoldenThread
\circ
SemanticTransduction
\circ
Summon

extracts an invariant and reinstantiates it in a new substrate.

SyntheticSensing
\circ
DeltaObservatory
\circ
RecursiveHindsight

reveals previously unresolved historical structure using newly available measurements.

Humility
\circ
RoleModulation
\circ
Goldilocks

prevents capability from becoming compulsive intervention.

The ordering matters.

In general:

A\circ B
\neq
B\circ A

Therefore Pantheon should eventually study operator sequencing, interference, amplification, cancellation, and stable compositions.

⸻

23. Semantic Field Discovery Engine

The next stage is not merely applying known operators.

It is discovering new ones.

The discovery engine should repeatedly:

\text{sample domains}
\rightarrow
\text{strip surface features}
\rightarrow
\text{compare transformations}
\rightarrow
\text{identify recurring structure}
\rightarrow
\text{propose invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{instantiate elsewhere}
\rightarrow
\text{test}

Successful invariants are promoted into the Pantheon.

Failed ones remain in provenance as counterexamples.

This converts Pantheon from an operator library into an operator-discovery architecture.

